{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966781,"nonce":"CNdM5VPot6yNOwYVfGGaQv9b7Nc=","title":"Two mounts of one component handed their widgets the same signal, because ctx-id resolution","message":"RESOLVED by 1138dc5d. `scoped_context_signal_id` asked the installed\nhandler via `deferred_ctx::with_installed`, which is gated on\n`IN_CONTEXT` — true only under `Deferred::run`. A render is not a\ndeferred body, so every bound prop fell through to the process-wide\ninstance and two mounts of one component bound one field. Fix: ask\n`installed_machine(fsm)` (the mount's own install) before the fallback.\n\n**What was NOT wrong:** the data boundary. A bound prop crosses as\n`Reactive::Signal` carrying the token, never a `Literal` snapshot, so\ntwo-way binding for extern widgets needs nothing built: the widget holds\nthe storage and `set` is the write. Do not build a registration\nprotocol, receivers or a binding registry — that was tried and reverted.\n\n**How it was found:** an `#[extern_widget]` probe with a `Reactive\u003ci32\u003e`\nprop that records the token it receives, mounted twice from a component\nowning its own `fsm`. Reasoning about the passes gave two wrong answers\nfirst; the probe gave the right one in a minute. `tests/\nbound_prop_per_instance.rs` is that probe, kept.\n\nThe remaining name-keyed step is `installed_machine(fsm)`; removing it\nneeds [[gotcha-effects-not-on-methods]].\n\n**Sibling, same session:** a mount's STATE was keyed by the FSM name\ntoo, so two mounts shared `:state` styling while holding separate\ncontexts. Fixed by keying the `SharedState\u003cFsmStateId\u003e` cell on the\nmachine (`fsm::state_for(name, machine)`). The old code's comment said\nkeying finer was impossible because a binding was made by the\nwhole-program wrap while a dispatch ran in a captured scope; that stopped\nbeing true once both sides resolve a machine the same way.","files":null}]}